[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Parallel Test Execution in JUnit 5 (Configuration, Thread Safety, and Resource Locking)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As test suites grow, execution time becomes a critical bottleneck. JUnit 5 introduces native support for parallel test execution, allowing tests to run concurrently and significantly reducing build times. However, running tests in parallel introduces challenges related to thread safety and shared resource contention."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Configuring Parallel Execution"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Parallel execution is disabled by default in JUnit 5. To enable it, you must create a configuration file named junit-platform.properties in your src/test/resources directory and set the execution.parallel.enabled property to true."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled = true"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also configure the execution mode for nodes (classes and methods). By default, classes and methods run sequentially even if parallel execution is enabled. You need to define a strategy:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.default = concurrent
junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.classes.default = concurrent"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Controlling Parallelism and Thread Pools"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit allows you to control how many threads are created during execution using a parallelism strategy. You can specify a dynamic configuration (based on CPU cores) or a fixed number of threads."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.config.strategy = dynamic
junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.config.dynamic.factor = 1.0"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"dynamic: Computes thread count based on the number of available processor cores multiplied by the factor."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"fixed: Uses a precise, hardcoded number of threads defined by junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.config.fixed.parallelism."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Thread Safety Considerations"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When multiple tests execute concurrently, they share the same JVM heap. Thread safety becomes a primary concern if tests mutate shared static state, singleton beans, or global configurations. To ensure thread safety:"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using mutable static fields inside test classes."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use thread-local variables (ThreadLocal) for state that must be isolated per test thread."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Design unit tests to be stateless and independent of execution order."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Resource Locking with @ResourceLock"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes tests must interact with a shared resource (such as a system property, a database table, or a file) that cannot be accessed concurrently. JUnit 5 provides the @ResourceLock annotation to synchronize access to these resources."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.parallel.ResourceLock;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.parallel.Resources;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.parallel.Execution;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.parallel.ExecutionMode;

@Execution(ExecutionMode.CONCURRENT)
public class DatabaseTests {

    @Test
    @ResourceLock(value = Resources.SYSTEM_PROPERTIES)
    public void testWithSystemPropertyOne() {
        System.setProperty("app.env", "staging");
        org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals("staging", System.getProperty("app.env"));
    }

    @Test
    @ResourceLock(value = Resources.SYSTEM_PROPERTIES)
    public void testWithSystemPropertyTwo() {
        System.setProperty("app.env", "production");
        org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals("production", System.getProperty("app.env"));
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, even though the execution mode is concurrent, the use of @ResourceLock with Resources.SYSTEM_PROPERTIES ensures that testWithSystemPropertyOne and testWithSystemPropertyTwo will not run at the same time, preventing race conditions on shared system properties."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Enabling parallel execution in JUnit 5 dramatically cuts down test suite execution times. By correctly tuning the junit-platform.properties file, designing stateless tests, and leveraging @ResourceLock for shared state, you can safely scale your testing infrastructure without introducing flaky tests."}]
